package helptopics

const sourcesTopic = `A host can deploy from several stack folders, each a source with its own clone, engine state, poll and deploy key. Commands on a stack need no source: a stack is named by its Compose project, and the service finds its folder.

Sources:
- qhost repo set <ssh-url> --dir <path> binds a further folder next to those bound earlier; a bound folder is never rebound to another URL, and folders may not lie inside each other (see qhost help repository).
- The id of a source comes from its path and never changes: /opt/stacks becomes opt-stacks, /srv/services/stacks becomes srv-services-stacks.
- Each subfolder of a source with a compose file is a stack. When the root of the source holds a compose file, found as in a subfolder, the source is exactly one stack instead: the whole folder except .qatlas/, named by its Compose project, else by the folder's name. Its subfolders are then part of it, and those with a compose file of their own get the finding ignored-stack-folder.
- Every remote source has its own deploy key: qhost key generate --dir <path> or qhost key set --dir <path> before qhost repo set (see qhost help credentials).
- A source whose fetch or poll fails keeps its state; the other sources go on. qhost status shows each source with its last poll, error and commits, and each stack with its source.
- The maintenance window and qhost stack updates cover the stacks of every source.

Taking over a folder:
- qhost source inspect <path> [--url <ssh-url>] classifies a folder and changes nothing: no file, permission, repository or container is touched, and no value of an env file is shown. --url is the SSH address to clone, or the one the folder must have as origin. It exits 0 for every case, a rejection included; read the case, or the case field of --output json.
- As root, inspect also shows missing (the folder does not exist yet), permissions (the number of paths and a summary of the changes source add would make, only when there are any) and effect, a fingerprint of the case, missing, the listed and left-out files and the permission changes. qhost source add --expect-effect <effect> applies only that: when the effect differs by then, it stops before it changes anything with the code state-changed, and you inspect again. The address, the stacks and the containers are not part of the effect. The choice of unclear files (--include of source inspect and source add, see below) is part of it: take the effect from inspect with the same --include, or add stops with state-changed.
- clone: the folder is missing or empty and would get a clone of the URL; without --url the answer says an address is needed.
- adopt-clone: the folder is a clone with an origin remote, and is taken over as it is; a --url must equal that origin.
- local: the folder is a repository without a remote and runs locally, never pushed. A --url is rejected here, as for a folder with files.
- files: the folder holds files and no repository. The answer lists the files a first commit would take and what it would leave out with the reason. A first commit takes only what the readable stacks of the folder refer to, read from their Compose files without Docker and without showing a value of .env: the Compose files, .env, env_file, label_file, configs file, build context and dockerfile, include, extends file and bind mounts of a file or folder that are read-only; variables in a path are resolved from .env, and one without a value is listed as not resolvable. Always taken: .gitignore, .gitattributes and the stack settings of the folder root. Left out: bind-mount (the data folder of a stack), not-referenced (no stack needs it, such as backup copies, notes and logs; a folder with only such files is one entry), writable-file (a single file a stack mounts writable, which may hold data), secrets-file (the file of a Compose secret), unreadable-stack (the stack cannot be read, so it brings no file; its finding names the file, and for a permission problem asks you to give the group of the deploy service read permission, which qhost never changes itself), too-large, not-regular (not a plain file or folder), unreadable and nested-repo (a folder with a repository of its own). Paths that leave the folder, absolute paths and links are never taken. source add takes it over (see below).
- reject: the folder cannot be taken over. The answer gives the reason and what to do, for example a symbolic link in the path, a folder inside another repository or overlapping another source, a repository with remotes but no origin, an address that differs from the origin, or a folder on a read-only file system.
- Every answer except a rejection shows the owner, group and mode of the folder (or of its nearest existing parent while it is missing), whether the service can write there, and whether permissions would have to be set. For a repository it also says whether the service can write its .git folder.
- For each stack the folder would bring it shows the functions and findings as qhost stack check does, and the containers that already run in the stack's folder: deploying the stack would create duplicate containers and take their ports twice. In the Docker engine of the service these are the containers of other Compose projects. In the rootless Docker engines of the host's users it is every container of a Compose project started from that folder, also under the stack's own project name, because the service does not see that engine. Each conflict names its engine: system, or rootless:<user>. qhost warns and does not block: the question before the takeover and before the approval of a proposal names the consequence, and the folder can still be taken over.
- The rootless engines are read only with sudo, and only through the socket of the user's own engine (/run/user/<uid>/docker.sock, never a link or a socket of another user) with one request for its container list. A user with rootless Docker set up whose engine is not running or does not answer, and every rootless engine when the command runs without sudo, is listed as not checked with the reason, and the rest of the answer still holds. Nothing is started, stopped or changed. In JSON each conflict has an engine field and the answer has engines_not_checked with engine and reason. When the stacks or the container engine cannot be read, the answer says why and the rest still holds.

Source add:
- qhost source add <path> [--url <ssh-url>] [--fingerprint <fp>] [--engine <engine>] [--yes] inspects the folder, shows the case and the changes it would make, and applies them only after you confirm: at a terminal by answering the question, without a terminal with --yes. A rejected folder is refused and nothing changes.
- clone and adopt-clone work like qhost repo set (see qhost help repository): the host key is confirmed at the terminal or with --fingerprint, and the folder needs its deploy key. --yes never confirms a host key.
- local binds the repository without a remote and without a deploy key. The service deploys the commits that people make there and rolls a failing one back with a revert commit in the same folder; nothing is pushed until qhost source remote gives it a remote.
- files shows the list of files and what is left out with the reason (the first 50 of each), and starts a repository only after you confirm: git init with branch main and core.sharedRepository=group, and a first commit of the listed files as qhost. Each path left out becomes a line of a block marked by qhost in the .gitignore of the folder, so a fresh clone ignores it too: a missing .gitignore is created, an existing one keeps its own lines and gets the block at its end (a block of an earlier takeover is extended, never repeated), and the first commit holds it. The preview shows the lines; with nothing to add no file in the folder is written, and the work tree is clean afterwards. A .gitignore that is a symbolic link, cannot be read or is larger than 1 MiB makes the folder rejected. The folder is then bound as local, or as remote with --url (see below). Permissions are set for the folder, the folders of listed files and the listed files. Before it changes anything, source add looks at the folder again and stops if the list or the left-out files differ from what was shown. Files left out are not in the repository: one a stack needs to read stays in the folder and must be readable by the service.
- Unclear files of a folder of files may be data or a secret: a file a stack mounts writable (writable-file, such as acme.json) and the file of a Compose secret (secrets-file). The preview lists them apart from the other left-out files, with the reason and the stack, and they stay out unless you choose them. At a terminal without --yes source add asks for each one (default no). --include <path> (repeatable, a path below the folder) takes a file into the first commit in advance, also with --yes and without a terminal; without a terminal and without --include the file stays out. A path that the service does not classify as unclear itself is refused and nothing changes, and so is a chosen file it cannot take (unreadable, too large, not a plain file). The chosen files are in the list that is confirmed and are marked there; a chosen file with a secret in it is taken like any other, and with --url the check of what a push would publish still applies to it. Only a folder of files has unclear files, and --include for another case is refused. source compare takes --include as well and lists the chosen files as included.
- Folder permissions are set only when the service cannot write the folder or its .git, as root and without changing an owner: the group of the service account (qhost-deploy, or the primary group of the user of the instance with --engine) for the folder, .git and every folder with versioned files; group write and setgid on those folders; group read and write on versioned files (for files, the listed ones); core.sharedRepository=group. Never changed: the sources of bind mounts and everything below them, other unversioned files, symbolic links, files with further hard links and paths outside the folder. Independent of that, an unversioned env file of a stack that the service cannot read (its secrets.env, or a file named by env_file that is not versioned) is listed in the preview as its own change, whether or not the service can write the folder: it gets the group of the service account and group read, and the owner and every other permission stay, so a secrets.env of mode 0600 becomes 0640. Content is never read. A Compose secret file, a file mounted writable, a link and a file with further hard links stay as they are.
- The folder is then added to the systemd drop-in and the service restarts once, as repo set does.
- --engine <engine> names the Docker engine of the folder: system (the default) or rootless:<user>, for a user whose deploy service instance is set up (qhost deploy-service setup --rootless <user>). The instance of that engine inspects, binds and deploys the folder, and the system engine does not start its stacks. A created folder gets that user as owner, the drop-in is the 10-stack-folders.conf of the unit qhost-deploy@<user>.service, and only that instance restarts. An engine without an instance is refused. The engine never changes silently: when containers of the folder run in another engine, source inspect and source add suggest it with the option.
- source inspect takes --engine too and inspects the folder as for that engine: the instance of the engine and its service account decide the permissions and the effect, and containers that run in that engine are no conflict. Pass the same --engine to source add together with --expect-effect.
- The terminal interface asks for the engine when it adds a folder or takes over a foreign project, as soon as the host has more than one deploy service: system and every engine with an instance are offered, and the engine where containers of the folder run is preselected, else system. Another engine than system makes it inspect the folder again for that engine. The confirmation names the engine, and the commands that run through sudo carry --engine. A host with one engine asks nothing.
- Folders and stack names stay unique across all engines. Before it changes anything, source add asks the deploy service of every other engine and refuses a folder that overlaps one of their stack folders and a stack name that one of them has. A service that cannot be asked is named as not checked, and the question then says the check is incomplete: confirm it with the terminal or --yes. repo set --dir asks the same for the folder, and then asks that question too (--yes).
- Every command on a stack or a stack folder reaches the deploy service of the engine that has it: qhost stack deploy, update, rollback, settings, secret, backup, datadir, check, changelog, updates, start, stop, restart, pause, unpause, pin, unpin and resume, qhost stack runs <stack>, qhost container logs --stack, qhost key --dir, qhost repo fetch --dir, qhost source remove and qhost source remote. Only a host with several deploy services is asked first, which runs without sudo for an administrator in the group qhost. When several engines have a stack of that name, for example after a commit added it to a second folder, the command stops and names them, and --engine system or --engine rootless:<user> chooses one; an engine that is not set up is refused. A deploy service that cannot be asked is a warning when the stack or folder is found in another engine, and otherwise the error names its engine. qhost stack update takes stacks of one engine per call. qhost stack runs without a stack reads the deploy service of the system engine, else the only instance, or the one --engine names; qhost stack log <run> asks every deploy service and reads the one that knows the run. qhost stack list, qhost source list and qhost status ask every deploy service and show the engine of each entry (system or rootless:<user>) as a column or the field engine; in qhost status the stacks and folders of an instance are keyed below instance.<user>. A deploy service that cannot be asked is a warning with its engine, not an error.
- qhost source list shows every bound folder of every deploy service with path, id, engine, kind, address (none for a local one), head, last poll, poll error and note.
- qhost source remove <path> [--yes] asks first. It releases only the binding and drops the engine state of the source: no file in the folder is deleted, no container is stopped, and the permissions, the drop-in entry and the deploy key stay, so source add binds the folder again. A source with an active run is refused.

Moving a project into a source:
- qhost source move <folder> --into <source> shows what moving the Compose project in <folder> into the existing source <source> would do, and does it after the question at a terminal or with --yes. Without --yes and without a terminal it is the preview only and changes nothing: no file is written, no container is touched, nothing is fetched or committed, and no value of an env file is shown. The folder must lie outside every source and hold one project with its compose file at its root; the project gets the folder <source>/<name of the folder>. Moving renames the folder on the same file system, so the data stays untouched and the way back is renaming it back. The deploy service of the engine that has <source> bound answers, and the command needs sudo like source inspect: the Docker engines of the host are read as there, and the deploy services of the other engines are asked for stack folders and stack names.
- The preview lists the files a commit into the source would take, chosen as for a folder of files (see above), what is left out with the reason, and the lines for the .gitignore of the folder. In JSON, movable is true exactly when blocks is empty; for the preview a block or a rejection is a complete answer and exits 0, with --yes a block exits 2 and nothing changes.
- A block means the move cannot be done as it is; each names its code, the reason and what to do. root-stack: the source is one root stack, a second stack does not fit. source-unreadable. target-exists: the target folder exists already. target-overlaps: it overlaps a stack folder of another source or engine. target-name: the folder name starts with a dot or has other characters than letters, digits, ., _ and -, so a move could not be taken back. other-filesystem: the folder and the source are on different file systems, so the folder cannot be renamed. engine-differs: containers of the project run in another Docker engine than the source (system or rootless:<user>); moving between engines is not supported. name-taken: a stack of that name exists in any engine. project-name-changes: the Compose project name would change (without name: or COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME it is the name of the folder), so a deploy would create new, empty named volumes; qhost renames nothing. absolute-bind-mount: a bind mount names a path inside the old folder as an absolute path. secrets-in-env: .env holds values of secret-like keys (PASSWORD, SECRET, TOKEN, KEY) and the source has a remote; the keys are named, never the values, and the fix is to move them to secrets.env by hand. folder-rejected, not-a-project, project-is-repository and stack-unreadable: the folder cannot be looked at as a project folder (it overlaps a source, lies in a repository, holds no compose file at its root or several stacks, has a repository of its own, or cannot be read).
- A warning does not block: secrets-in-env for a local source (they are committed; a remote can be given later only after they are moved), fixed-tags (images with a fixed tag cannot be updated by qhost, the stack counts as deploy only; qhost stack check shows the finding), downtime (the containers are recreated from the new folder) and not-checked (an engine or deploy service could not be asked, or the containers could not be read; without sudo no rootless engine is asked).
- qhost never rewrites a compose file or .env to make a project fit: what does not fit is named, and you change it yourself.
- The move runs as root in this order: down (docker compose down in the old folder through the deploy service of the engine, without -v, so named volumes stay), rename (the folder into the source on the same file system; never over something at the target and never through a symbolic link), permissions (the group of the service account with group write on the folder, the folders of the listed files and the listed files, as source add does; owner, bind-mount sources and unlisted files stay), commit (the listed files and the .gitignore lines in one commit as the person behind sudo, pushed when the source has a remote) and deploy (the normal run of the new stack with its check; the command waits for its result). Data is never copied or deleted.
- The folder lies in the source uncommitted only between rename and commit, and the head the commit builds on is read after the rename. Meanwhile the working copy counts as changed, so the source skips its fast-forwards instead of attempting them and catches up right after the commit; a commit whose remote branch moved catches up itself, which git refuses only when the remote added the same folder, and then nothing is committed.
- When a step fails, the move is taken back as far as it got: the folder is renamed back first, the containers of the project are removed once the deploy was tried, a commit that reached the branch is taken back by a revert commit that is pushed (only once the folder is out of the source, so the working copy following it deletes no file), group and mode of each path and the .gitignore are put back as they were, and a project that ran starts again in its old folder with docker compose up -d. A failed first run of the new stack stays in qhost stack runs, and qhost status lists the stack as removed from the source.
- The output lists every step and every step of the way back with its result. result is moved, rolled-back or rollback-incomplete, location says where the folder is, commit, revert and run name what was made; JSON adds moved, steps, rollback and remaining. A move that was taken back exits 1 and says why. When a step of the way back fails, the others still run unless they need it (renaming back, or a run that did not end), and remaining and the error say exactly what is left to do by hand.

Remote for a folder:
- qhost source remote <path> <ssh-url> [--fingerprint <fp>] [--yes] gives a local source a remote repository. qhost source add <path> --url <ssh-url> does it for a folder of files in one step, but only for a repository that already holds commits; take the folder over without --url first for an empty one. The host key is confirmed as for repo set and --yes never confirms it. The folder needs its deploy key first, also while it is a local source (qhost key generate --dir <path> or qhost key set --dir <path>), with write access to the repository (see qhost help credentials).
- qhost source compare <path> <ssh-url> [--fingerprint <fp>] [--include <path>] shows that comparison alone, with the host key confirmed as for repo set and the secret-like files as an answer rather than an error; it changes nothing in the folder and pins the confirmed host key. --expect-state <empty|equal|different> and --expect-commit <commit> of source remote and of source add --url (a folder of files) apply exactly the comparison shown: the commit is empty exactly when the state is empty, and when the fresh comparison differs they stop before any change with the code state-changed.
- Both compare the folder with the branch of the remote and show the state before anything changes. empty (source remote only): the local history is pushed as the branch. equal (the same files): the history of the remote is taken over and no commit is made; the former local history stays under refs/qhost/local-before-remote. different: the paths that differ are listed (the first 50 and their number), and the state of the host becomes one commit on the remote branch and is pushed, or you cancel. The host never overwrites the remote and nothing is forced.
- For a folder of files, the comparison plans the files again with what the remote branch versions: a file of the folder that the remote versions is taken even when no stack refers to it (a README, or a compose file in a folder that is no stack) and is marked as versioned in the remote, so joining removes nothing the remote has, and a folder with the files of the remote stays equal. Everything the remote does not version is chosen as for a folder without --url. A file left out for another reason, such as writable-file, secrets-file, bind-mount, unreadable-stack or unreadable, stays out even when the remote versions it, unless it is a writable-file or secrets-file that you chose with --include. This plan is what you confirm.
- After your confirmation the service acts only on the state and commit that were shown and changes nothing if the remote moved. The source is then remote; for a folder of files the repository starts on the history of the remote.
- Secret check: every commit the push would publish is checked for secrets files, env files with values and private keys. A finding stops the command with secrets-found before anything changes. It names commit, path and kind, never a value, and cannot be overridden: clean the history yourself, then run the command again.

Uncommitted changes of a stack folder:
- A change in a stack folder that is not committed keeps its stack out of every run and holds back the fast-forward of the whole stack folder; it shows as the finding local-changes, and in the TUI the row of the folder names it. = on that row or in the file browser of the folder, or a click on its note, opens the view Changes: the list of source changes; enter shows the diff of the marked file, coloured by added and removed lines, as root directly and otherwise after a question through sudo qhost source diff; d discards the marked file after a question through sudo qhost source discard, which names a new file as deleted; > commits every listed file in one commit at the head they were read at through sudo qhost source commit; started as root the interface calls the service itself. A refusal of the commit says why in words, and its result offers the area Runs, where the run of the stacks starts. Edits through the settings also stop with it. Saving a file in the file browser of the TUI writes it without a commit, so it counts the same way, except secrets.env and the data folders, which are not versioned.
- qhost source changes <path> lists those changes for all stacks of the bound folder in one list, with the path below the folder, the kind (modified, new, which is not versioned yet or added, or deleted) and the stack. The same files count as for the finding: secrets.env, the data folder of a stack and new files below a bind mount of a stack are the host's own and never appear; a changed versioned file below a bind mount does. An administrator in the group qhost may run it without sudo.
- qhost source diff <path> <file> shows the unified diff of one listed file against the last commit, a new file against nothing. It shows file contents that an administrator may not be allowed to read, so only root runs it. The text is cut after 256 KiB and the answer says so.
- sudo qhost source discard <path> <file>... [--yes] asks first (default no) and then takes back the changes at the named files: a modified or deleted versioned file is restored from the last commit, a new file is removed because its path is named. Every file must be exactly one that source changes lists. A folder, a pattern, a path outside the stack folder, a link out of it or a file the host owns is refused, and then nothing at all is changed. The edit cannot be brought back afterwards. A stack of the files with an active run is refused.
- sudo qhost source commit <path> <file>... --head <commit> commits exactly the named files, deletions included, as the folder has them now, and nothing else. --head is the full commit id the changes were read at (the head of qhost source list); when the branch moved since, nothing is committed and it stops with head-moved. Every file must be one that source changes lists, or it stops with not-a-change, and every stack of the files must still load. With a remote the commit is scanned for secret-like files, which stop it, and pushed as a plain fast-forward: when the remote branch moved, the files are committed on its new head once if they are still exactly the same changes, and a remote that refuses the push, such as a protected branch, is reported with its reason. A push that does not land stops with push-rejected, and nothing is committed. The commit names the files and the person behind sudo. A stack of the files with an active run is refused.
- When nothing else is left, the finding goes at the next poll, which starts as soon as discard or commit is done, and the stack is rolled out again. The commands reach the deploy service of the engine that has the folder bound; --engine names one instead. --output json gives the lists as data.

Foreign Compose projects:
- A Compose project that was started outside qhost shows in the Containers area of the TUI as a foreign project, with the folder and the Compose and env files its containers were started with. a on its group, one of its containers or its detail opens the adoption: it only inspects the folder, shows the project, the files from the labels and what works with them, and goes on with the questions of the case (local, files or adopt-clone) as qhost source add does. Nothing changes before the last yes. Where a source of the same engine that is no root stack exists, a first asks whether the project becomes a source of its own or moves into one of those sources: then the preview of qhost source move follows with the target folder, files, blocks with what to do and warnings, a block ends the flow, and the last question shows the command sudo qhost source move --into <source> --yes (run directly as root) and the result with the way back.
- The folder becomes a source of exactly one stack: its compose file must lie directly in the folder. The adoption is refused, with the cause, when the containers carry no absolute working directory, when a Compose or env file lies outside that folder, when the inspection rejects the folder or does not find exactly one stack.
- A warning, which does not block, appears when the project name or the Compose or env files of the running containers differ from those a deploy would pass to Compose: the first deploy then creates new containers instead of taking over the running ones.
- Without the TUI, qhost container list shows the folder and the Compose files, and qhost source add <folder> takes the folder over.

Commits of people in a local folder:
- A person commits as a member of the group qhost-deploy (usermod -aG qhost-deploy <user>, then a new login) or as the service account with sudo -u qhost-deploy git -c safe.directory=<path> .... The folder belongs to another user, so git refuses it until it is marked safe; a group member does that once with git config --global --add safe.directory <path>.
- A person who creates new folders should work with umask 002: a folder made with the usual umask 022 is not group-writable, and the service could not write into it when a revert has to restore files there.

Stack names:
- Compose tells stacks apart only by project name, so a name is deployed from one source alone. When two sources list the same name, the stack already deployed keeps it, else the one of the source bound first; the other gets the finding name-collision and is never deployed until one of them gets another project name.
- qhost status lists such a stack as source.<id>.stack.<name>; the stack that holds the name stays stack.<name>.

Earlier installations:
- A host bound to one repository by an earlier version keeps running: at its first start the service makes the binding its first source, moves the engine state below it and keeps the existing deploy key. Nothing needs to be done.`
